 "Q3c_text": "Fracture starts at fastener 1 (top), which carries the largest tension from the opening of the joint about a heel near the bottom of the rod footprint; fasteners 2 and 6 follow within tens of microseconds, then 3 and 5, fastener 4 last (it is at the heel and sees mostly shear). Fracture is tension-dominated plastic elongation of the threaded shank; once the top three go, the joint unzips in the same impact. Fractures occur 0.4-1.5 ms after the start of the pulse.",
 "Q4_block_or_keensert_fracture": {
  "answer": 0,
  "basis": "judgment, not modelled: steel Keenserts in a 4 in aluminium block are stronger than the 10-32 threads"
 },
 "Q5_cantilever_fracture": {
  "answer": 0,
  "basis": "judgment: the reduced model predicts plastic bending of the flange and yielding at the rod root, no fracture model; low confidence"
 },
